\title{Exact Intersections and Local Decomposition\\in Linear Intersecting Hypergraphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 31, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop the elementary structural theory underlying the Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz coloring problem in its linear-hypergraph formulation. A finite hypergraph is linear when distinct edges share at most one vertex and intersecting when every two edges meet. We prove that distinct edges in a linear intersecting hypergraph meet in exactly one vertex, so fixing an edge assigns every other edge to a unique point on it. Two distinct edges through the same vertex become disjoint after that vertex is removed. Inclusion--exclusion then gives $\card{E\cup F}+1=\card E+\card F$; in an $r$-uniform system this specializes to $\card{E\cup F}=2r-1$. We formulate rainbow coloring as edgewise injectivity and explain how the decomposition supports incidence algorithms, exact degree counts, clique-union coloring, and future matching and probabilistic approaches.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A hypergraph permits a single constraint to involve more than two objects. Its vertices are objects and its edges are finite groups of vertices. A rainbow coloring assigns labels so that every edge has pairwise distinct colors. This is the natural hypergraph counterpart of proper graph coloring after each hyperedge is replaced by a clique.

The Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz problem asks for an optimal coloring in a rigid overlap regime. In one standard formulation, there are $k$ edges, each containing $k$ vertices, and any two distinct edges share at most one vertex. The desired conclusion is that $k$ colors suffice. The lower bound is immediate from any edge of size $k$; the substance lies in showing that interactions among edges force no additional color.

This paper establishes the local structural layer in the intersecting regime. Linearity places an upper bound on overlap, intersectingness places a lower bound, and uniformity optionally fixes all edge sizes. Together, the first two assumptions force exact pairwise overlap. This yields unique contact points, a partition relative to a fixed edge, disjoint residual branches around a vertex, and exact union formulas. These invariants support efficient recognition algorithms and provide the low-complexity interfaces across which a global coloring must be coordinated.

\section{Definitions and viewpoints}

\begin{definition}
Let $V$ be a set. A \emph{finite hypergraph} on $V$ is a finite collection $\Hcal$ of finite subsets of $V$. Members of $V$ are vertices and members of $\Hcal$ are edges. The hypergraph is \emph{$r$-uniform} if $\card E=r$ for every $E\in\Hcal$. It is \emph{linear} if $\card{E\cap F}\leq 1$ whenever $E,F\in\Hcal$ are distinct, and \emph{intersecting} if $E\cap F\neq\varnothing$ for every $E,F\in\Hcal$.
\end{definition}

For a positive integer $q$, write $[q]=\set{1,\ldots,q}$.

\begin{definition}
A map $c:V\to[q]$ is a \emph{proper rainbow coloring} of $\Hcal$ if $c(x)\neq c(y)$ whenever $x\neq y$ lie in a common edge. The hypergraph is \emph{$q$-colorable} if such a map exists.
\end{definition}

The \emph{clique-union graph} $G(\Hcal)$ has active vertex set $\bigcup\Hcal$, with distinct vertices adjacent precisely when they lie together in some edge. A rainbow coloring of $\Hcal$ is exactly a proper graph coloring of $G(\Hcal)$. Thus the hypergraph and clique-union formulations have identical chromatic content.

\section{Coloring as edgewise injectivity}

\begin{theorem}[Edgewise Injectivity]
If $c:V\to[q]$ is a proper rainbow coloring of a finite hypergraph $\Hcal$, then $c|_E:E\to[q]$ is injective for every $E\in\Hcal$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $x,y\in E$ and suppose $c(x)=c(y)$. If $x\neq y$, properness requires $c(x)\neq c(y)$, a contradiction. Hence $x=y$.
\end{proof}

The converse follows directly from the definitions, so rainbow properness and edgewise injectivity are equivalent.

\begin{corollary}[Complete Palette]
If $\Hcal$ is $r$-uniform and $c:V\to[r]$ is a proper rainbow coloring, then every edge contains exactly one vertex of each color.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The restricted map on an edge is an injection between two finite sets of cardinality $r$, hence a bijection.
\end{proof}

Optimal coloring can therefore be viewed as the coordination of local bijections from edges to one common palette. Shared vertices force the local assignments to agree.

\section{Unique pairwise contact}

\begin{lemma}[Uniqueness Under Linearity]
Let $\Hcal$ be linear, and let $E,F\in\Hcal$ be distinct. If $x,y\in E\cap F$, then $x=y$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $x\neq y$, then $\set{x,y}\subseteq E\cap F$, giving $\card{E\cap F}\geq2$, contrary to linearity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique-Contact Theorem]
Let $\Hcal$ be finite, linear, and intersecting. For distinct $E,F\in\Hcal$, there exists a unique vertex $x$ such that $x\in E\cap F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Intersectingness makes $E\cap F$ nonempty, and the preceding lemma makes any two of its elements equal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact-Intersection Theorem]
If $\Hcal$ is finite, linear, and intersecting, then
\[
\card{E\cap F}=1
\]
for all distinct $E,F\in\Hcal$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Intersectingness gives the lower bound $1\leq\card{E\cap F}$, while linearity gives the upper bound $\card{E\cap F}\leq1$.
\end{proof}

Fix $E\in\Hcal$, and for $x\in E$ define
\[
\Hcal_x(E)=\set{F\in\Hcal:F\neq E\text{ and }x\in F}.
\]

\begin{corollary}[Contact Partition]
If $\Hcal$ is linear and intersecting, the families $\Hcal_x(E)$, indexed by $x\in E$, partition $\Hcal\setminus\set E$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Every $F\neq E$ has one unique point $x\in E\cap F$, so it belongs to exactly one indexed family.
\end{proof}

Thus every other edge acquires a canonical address on the reference edge.

\section{Punctured edges and local decomposition}

\begin{theorem}[Punctured-Edge Disjointness]
Let $\Hcal$ be a finite linear hypergraph. If distinct edges $E,F\in\Hcal$ both contain $x$, then
\[
(E\setminus\set x)\cap(F\setminus\set x)=\varnothing.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A point $y$ in the displayed intersection would satisfy $y\neq x$ and $x,y\in E\cap F$, contradicting linearity.
\end{proof}

Let $\Hcal(x)=\set{E\in\Hcal:x\in E}$ and $d(x)=\card{\Hcal(x)}$.

\begin{corollary}[Disjoint Star Decomposition]
In a finite linear hypergraph, the sets $E\setminus\set x$ for $E\in\Hcal(x)$ are pairwise disjoint, and
\[
\bigcup_{E\in\Hcal(x)}E
=
\set{x}\,\mathbin{\dot\cup}
\mathop{\dot\bigcup}_{E\in\Hcal(x)}(E\setminus\set x).
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Pairwise disjointness follows from the theorem. None of the punctured edges contains $x$, and adjoining $x$ reconstructs every incident edge.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniform Star Count]
If $\Hcal$ is also $r$-uniform, then
\[
\card{\bigcup_{E\in\Hcal(x)}E}=1+d(x)(r-1).
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Each punctured edge has $r-1$ vertices, and all displayed pieces are disjoint.
\end{proof}

Every additional edge through $x$ therefore forces $r-1$ new vertices into the union of the star. This is the basic local source of degree estimates.

\section{Exact two-edge cardinalities}

For arbitrary finite sets, inclusion--exclusion gives
\[
\card{E\cup F}+\card{E\cap F}=\card E+\card F.
\]
Exact intersection turns this into a fixed identity.

\begin{theorem}[Two-Edge Union Identity]
Let $\Hcal$ be finite, linear, and intersecting. If $E,F\in\Hcal$ are distinct, then
\[
\card{E\cup F}+1=\card E+\card F,
\]
or equivalently $\card{E\cup F}=\card E+\card F-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The Exact-Intersection Theorem gives $\card{E\cap F}=1$. Substitute this value into inclusion--exclusion.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform Two-Edge Union Formula]
Let $\Hcal$ be an $r$-uniform finite linear intersecting hypergraph. For distinct $E,F\in\Hcal$,
\[
\card{E\cup F}=2r-1.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The preceding theorem gives $\card{E\cup F}+1=\card E+\card F=2r$.
\end{proof}

The formula is also diagnostic. A smaller union reveals multiple shared vertices, while a union of size $2r$ reveals disjointness.

\begin{example}
Consider
\[
E_1=\set{0,1,2},\qquad E_2=\set{0,3,4},\qquad E_3=\set{1,3,5}.
\]
Every pair intersects in one point, so the system is $3$-uniform, linear, and intersecting. Every pairwise union has $5=2\cdot3-1$ vertices. The assignment
\[
c(0)=1,\ c(1)=2,\ c(2)=3,\ c(3)=3,\ c(4)=2,\ c(5)=1
\]
gives every edge all three colors exactly once.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}

Let $n=\card\Hcal$, let $m=\card{\bigcup\Hcal}$, and let $L=\sum_{E\in\Hcal}\card E$. Assume hash-set storage.

\subsection{Pairwise structural audit}

For each unordered pair $\set{E,F}$, compute $s=\card{E\cap F}$. The system is linear and intersecting exactly when $s=1$ for every pair. Uniformity is checked by comparing all edge sizes. Once these facts hold, $\card{E\cup F}=2r-1$ supplies a redundant but useful consistency invariant. Naive intersection costs
\[
O\!\left(\sum_{i<j}\min(\card{E_i},\card{E_j})\right),
\]
which is $O(n^2r)$ for $r$-uniform input.

\subsection{Contact-map construction}

Fix $E$. Create one bucket for each $x\in E$. For each $F\neq E$, compute $E\cap F$. A singleton $\set x$ places $F$ in the bucket of $x$; an empty or larger intersection reports failure. The cost is
\[
O\!\left(\sum_{F\neq E}\min(\card E,\card F)\right),
\]
or $O(nr)$ in the uniform case. Each nonreference edge is stored once.

\subsection{Star-disjointness audit}

Build an incidence map from vertices to containing edges in expected time $O(L)$. At a center $x$, scan all vertices of $E\setminus\set x$ over incident edges $E$. A repeated vertex exhibits two edges sharing both $x$ and another point. Auditing every center takes
\[
O\!\left(\sum_{E\in\Hcal}\card E^2\right),
\]
which is $O(nr^2)$ for $r$-uniform input.

\subsection{Illustrative coloring search}

For small examples, backtracking assigns one of $q$ colors to each active vertex and rejects a proposed color if it repeats within an incident edge. High-conflict vertices should be chosen first. The worst case remains exponential, $O(q^m)$; this is an exploratory routine, not a polynomial proof of the general chromatic statement. Structural audits should precede search so that computed examples are interpreted in the intended regime.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\paragraph{Clique-union coloring.}
Replacing every edge by a clique preserves the proper colorings. In an intersecting linear family, two generating cliques share exactly one vertex. Every pairwise interface between local clique colorings is therefore one consistency constraint. The contact partition records where those constraints attach to a selected clique.

\paragraph{Resource and frequency assignment.}
If edges are sets of mutually interfering devices, valid frequency assignments are injections on every group. Groups meeting at a hub have otherwise disjoint memberships. An $r$-uniform star of degree $d(x)$ contains exactly $1+d(x)(r-1)$ devices, allowing capacity to be counted without hidden duplication.

\paragraph{Block designs.}
When vertices are treatments and edges are experimental blocks, exact pairwise intersection means every two blocks share one treatment. Two blocks of size $r$ cover $2r-1$ treatments, and the contact partition sorts all blocks by the treatment they share with a reference block.

\paragraph{Incidence matrices and transversals.}
In the $0$--$1$ incidence matrix, the scalar product of rows for $E$ and $F$ equals $\card{E\cap F}$. In the present regime every two distinct rows have scalar product one. A color class meets each row in at most one position, and in an optimal $r$-coloring it meets each row exactly once. This suggests systems of distinct representatives and partial transversals as bridges to the global coloring problem.

\section{Discussion}

The results convert qualitative overlap assumptions into canonical data. The edge-centered view partitions other edges by unique contact points. The vertex-centered view decomposes stars into disjoint punctured branches. The pair-centered view says that exactly one vertex is duplicated when two edges are counted together.

Pairwise exactness does not make all residual pieces globally disjoint. Edges meeting a reference edge at different points may intersect one another elsewhere, creating the global dependency network. Nor do individually valid local palettes automatically assemble into a global coloring. That unresolved coordination is where matching theory, list coloring, and probabilistic methods enter. The present results establish the exact local arithmetic and structure; they do not claim that these facts alone prove the full $k$-color conclusion.

\section{Future work}

Natural next steps are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item prove the full Erd\H{o}s--Faber--Lov\'asz theorem for finite linear hypergraphs with $k$ edges of size $k$ in the rainbow-edge formulation;
\item develop partial transversals and systems of distinct representatives for the incidence matrix, connecting them to list coloring of the clique-union graph;
\item analyze random partial colorings, concentration bounds, and a Lov\'asz-local-lemma completion step for sparse or high-degree regimes;
\item develop the dual formulation using $k$ pairwise-intersecting cliques of order $k$, any two sharing at most one vertex; and
\item study finite-projective-plane instances and natural incidence colorings that attain the expected bound.
\end{enumerate}
Computational extensions include canonical encodings up to isomorphism, generators of small linear intersecting hypergraphs, exact chromatic search, and visual incidence explorers.

\section{Conclusion}

A finite hypergraph that is both linear and intersecting has rigid pairwise geometry. Distinct edges meet in one unique vertex. Relative to any fixed edge, all others have unique contact addresses. Through a fixed vertex, punctured incident edges are pairwise disjoint. Inclusion--exclusion gives
\[
\card{E\cup F}+1=\card E+\card F,
\]
and uniformity sharpens this to $\card{E\cup F}=2r-1$. Meanwhile, proper rainbow coloring is precisely injectivity on each edge, becoming a local bijection when an $r$-edge uses $r$ colors. Unique contacts, disjoint branches, exact unions, and compatible edgewise palettes form the local vocabulary from which global coloring arguments proceed.

