# Explore Exact Intersections

Begin with the central geometric idea: in a linear intersecting hypergraph, every distinct pair of edges has **one unique contact point**. Use the explorer to choose a reference edge and watch all other edges sort themselves by that contact.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The same structure can be checked computationally. The full audit below reports uniformity, exact intersections, pairwise union sizes, punctured stars, and sample rainbow colorings.

{{demo:0}}

Three focused computations isolate the pairwise census, the punctured-star decomposition, and edgewise color injectivity.

{{demo:1}}

{{demo:2}}

{{demo:3}}

## From contact to counting

Once the intersection has size one, finite inclusion–exclusion becomes the exact law $|E\cup F|=|E|+|F|-1$. In the uniform case this is $2r-1$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the proof of the exact union law</summary>
For finite sets, $|E\cup F|+|E\cap F|=|E|+|F|$. Intersectingness gives at least one shared vertex; linearity gives at most one. Hence $|E\cap F|=1$, and substitution proves the formula. If both edges have size $r$, the right side is $2r$.
</details>

## Inspecting the geometry

{{visualization:0}}

The pairwise audit is the simplest recognition procedure. It checks every unordered edge pair and records any intersection whose size differs from one.

{{algorithm:0}}

Fixing one edge yields a sharper data structure: the unique-contact map.

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the contact-partition argument</summary>
Every nonreference edge meets the reference edge because the hypergraph is intersecting. It cannot meet it twice because the hypergraph is linear. Therefore it belongs to exactly one contact bucket.
</details>

## Stars, branches, and local growth

At a vertex $x$, remove $x$ from every incident edge. The remaining branches are pairwise disjoint. For uniform rank $r$ and degree $d(x)$, their union therefore has $1+d(x)(r-1)$ vertices.

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why punctured branches are disjoint</summary>
If two punctured branches shared $y$, their original edges would share both $x$ and $y$. Since $x\ne y$, that would contradict linearity.
</details>

## Coordinate the palette

A rainbow coloring is simply a map that is injective on every edge. When the edge and palette both have size $r$, the restriction is a bijection: every color appears once.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

For further background, see the [hypergraph overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypergraph), the [Erdős–Faber–Lovász conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93Faber%E2%80%93Lov%C3%A1sz_conjecture), and the [inclusion–exclusion principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inclusion%E2%80%93exclusion_principle).

